 ## Interactive API
 
 Beyond static SVG, the engine exposes the geometry it draws, so GUI apps can paint it with any framework and let users drag it.
 
 ### Scene, drag and re-route
 
-- **`scene::scene(&graph)`** returns a `Scene`: the position, size and shape of every node plus the path of every edge, in final coordinates, ready to draw with any painter. A flowchart or state edge drawn on the axes (see [Edges](#edges)) comes as `SceneEdge::waypoints`, each corner flanked by points 14 px either side (half the shorter adjoining leg where a leg is under 28 px, as on a labelled edge's short first leg, from its source to its step), which a painter's spline turns through as a curve of about 8 px radius (tighter at such a corner); its `SceneEdge::bezier` is only a fallback that does not follow the path (a cubic whose controls only roughly follow the path: where the axis stage put the first and last corners, or for a back-edge lane the ends of its run along the lane; or a straight line for an edge the router rerouted, its channel nudge redrew or its spacing pass moved clear. Its ends lie on the end nodes' outlines, usually but not always at the path's ends: a fork-to-join edge, or an end a later stage moved, can sit tens of px along the side. Read `waypoints[0]` and `waypoints.last()` for the drawn ends, and the first and last legs for the end tangents and arrowhead direction). An edge with empty `waypoints` (a straight edge, a self-loop) is its single cubic `bezier`, unless it sets a non-default mermaid `curve` (`SceneEdge::meta.curve`): that curve is then drawn between the bezier's two ends (through all four of its points for a self-loop); an edge with waypoints and a `curve` draws that curve through them. A static `Scene`'s `width` × `height` and node centres are exactly the SVG's canvas and coordinates. Nodes and edges keep a stable identity (`SceneNode.id`, `SceneEdge.from`/`to`).
+- **`scene::scene(&graph)`** returns a `Scene`: the position, size and shape of every node plus the path of every edge, in final coordinates, ready to draw with any painter. A static `Scene`'s `width` × `height` and node centres are exactly the SVG's canvas and coordinates. Nodes and edges keep a stable identity (`SceneNode.id`, `SceneEdge.from` / `to`).
 - **`scene::route(&graph, &positions)`** re-routes every edge for custom node positions (a user's drag) *without* re-running layout, so nodes never jump back.
-- **`scene::route_partial`** re-routes only the edges a move can affect, keeping the rest of a base drawing: a drag frame costs what lies near the drag, not the whole drawing (see [Interactive editing](#interactive-editing)). `scene::box_edge_bezier` gives flowmaid's curve for one edge between two freely placed boxes, for hosts that own their node model.
+- **`scene::route_partial`** re-routes only the edges a move can affect, keeping the rest of a base drawing, so a drag frame costs what lies near the drag (see [Interactive editing](#interactive-editing)). It combs its small fans only; the larger ones are combed when the drag ends and `route()` runs.
+- **`scene::box_edge_bezier`** gives flowmaid's curve for one edge between two freely placed boxes, for hosts that own their node model.
 - **`scene::to_svg(&scene)`** exports any state, including after drags. `render()` is just a wrapper over the same pipeline, so the SVG and the interactive flow always agree.
-- **Edge ends.** An edge ends on its node's border with its end tangent square to the side, so a host draws its own arrowhead there (an end alone on its side meets it in the middle, three or more ends on one side are spaced evenly, and a nearly straight 1:1 edge is drawn straight). flowmaid's own SVG stops the stroke half a head short under its 11px head and ends a curve still turning there in a straight run along that tangent, within a pixel of the curve outside the head — painter-only treatments that leave `SceneEdge::bezier` as it is. So are the hops and gaps it draws where edges cross (see [Edges](#edges)): `SceneEdge::waypoints` hold the plain path, and a host that paints the scene itself draws its crossings its own way.
-- **Links.** Nodes with a mermaid `click` carry it on `SceneNode::link` (url, target, tooltip), so a host can open the link or show the tooltip itself.
 - **Compact layout.** `fold::scene_compact` folds a long linear chain into balanced rows that fit a viewport (CLI `--compact <px>`).
+- **Edge paths.** A flowchart or state edge drawn on the axes (see [Edges](#edges)) comes as `SceneEdge::waypoints`, which a painter's spline turns through as rounded corners. Read the drawn ends from `waypoints[0]` and `waypoints.last()`, and the arrowhead direction from the first and last legs. `SceneEdge::bezier` does not follow such a path: it is the edge only when `waypoints` is empty (a straight edge, a self-loop).
+- **Curves.** A non-default mermaid `curve` is in `SceneEdge::meta.curve`. It is drawn between the bezier's two ends, or through the waypoints when the edge has them.
+- **Edge ends.** An edge ends on its node's border, square to the side, so a host draws its own arrowhead there. Trimming the stroke under the arrowhead, and the hops and gaps at crossings, are done by flowmaid's SVG painter only: the waypoints hold the plain path.
+- **Links.** Nodes with a mermaid `click` carry it on `SceneNode::link` (url, target, tooltip), so a host can open the link or show the tooltip itself.
+- **Real fonts.** Embedders using a real font can inject its metrics. Size nodes with `layout::intrinsic_size_wrapped(node, g.wrapping_width(), measure)` and pass the same `measure` to `scene::scene_sized_with` / `scene::route_sized_with`. `Graph::set_wrapping_width` sets the wrap width (`None` = never wrap). `scene_sized` / `route_sized` break lines with the built-in estimate, as markdown labels always do.
 
